R"VG(
# sermon_everyday.vg (H-04; the second pass 2026-10-10): the sermon's register for the everyday (a fire, a storm, a plague of the fields,
# a wonder, a trial, a law, a disaster of the works, a fair, the games, the ruler's visit, a price, envoys, a new play): what happened,
# then what the preacher makes of it (`med.ser.t.*`: the turns shared by the kinds, each in the people's own goods), the words a sermon
# says them with, and the sermon's news when there is none.

sermon.fire
  {med.ser.fire} {med.ser.t.help} {=.told}
  (0.6) {med.ser.fire} {med.ser.t.lesson} {=.told}
med.ser.fire
  ($e.what=granary) The granary at {$e.place} burned, and the {crop} with it.
  ($e.what=temple) Our sister house at {$e.place} burned; {god} was not in the roof, whatever they say.
  ($e.what=market) The market at {$e.place} burned, stalls and all, and the {med.chr.trades} have nothing to sell.
  ($e.what=street) Fire took a street at {$e.place}, {#$e.value|house} of it.
  ($e.what=half) Half of {$e.place} burned, and the other half is sleeping in their neighbours' houses.
  ($e.what=grass|$e.what=forest) The fire went over the hills by {$e.place}, and the {bird} came down into the town ahead of it.
  ($e.what=library) The books burned at {$e.place}, and what was in them is in someone's head now, or nowhere.
  ($e.what=palace|$e.what=harbour|$e.what=mill|$e.what=works|$e.what=tower|$e.what=camp) There was a fire at {$e.place}, and it took {#$e.value|house} before the bucket line could stop it.
  The fire at {$e.place} began in {med.ser.firebegan} and ended in {#$e.value|house}.
med.ser.firebegan
  the {craft}-house
  a bakery
  a hayloft
  a lamp left burning
  a quarrel

# the turns the kinds share
med.ser.t.help (share=0.4)
  Help them; that is the whole sermon.
  Take {food} and blankets, and do not stay to talk.
  Whoever has {crop} put by, this is what it was for.
  Some of you have {material} in your yards. You know what to do with it.
  The {med.chr.trades} will rebuild; carry the {material} for them.
  Find room under your {roofs} for the ones who have none.
  Keep a {vessel.one.n} of water by your own door tonight, and every night.
  Lend them {tool}, and do not count the days.
  They will need {fuel} before {season.next}; the rest of us can spare it.
med.ser.t.lesson (share=0.5)
  {god} did not light it. Somebody did, carelessly, and somebody else will.
  A fire is a sermon nobody needs to hear twice.
  It is not a judgement; it is {weather} and a careless hand.
  We build in {walls.of} and roof in {material}; we should not be surprised.
  The old ones built further apart. Now we know why.

sermon.storm
  {med.ser.storm} {med.ser.t.storm} {=.told}
med.ser.storm
  (!has:$e.place) The storm went over us all, the just and the others, and did not ask which was which.
  (!has:$e.place) After the storm the whole street was out on the roofs, quarrelling about whose tiles were whose.
  ($e.what=hail has:$e.place) The hail came down on the {crop} at {$e.place} like stones from a sling.
  ($e.what=wind has:$e.place) The wind took the roofs off at {$e.place}, {roofs} and all.
  ($e.what=snow) The snow came early and deep, and the {herd} were lost on the hills.
  ($e.what=dust) The dust came in under every door, and we ate it with the bread.
  ($e.what=sea has:$e.place) The sea came up in the storm and took the boats at {$e.place}, and some of the men with them.
  ($e.what=lightning has:$e.place) Lightning struck at {$e.place} and split the old {tree.n} there.
  ($e.what=rain) It rained until {river} forgot where its banks were.
med.ser.t.storm (share=0.5)
  It was not aimed. It only felt aimed.
  Some of you have roofs to spare; you know what to do.
  Look in on the old ones; they cannot clear their doorways of {weather} the way you can.
  Dry your neighbour's bedding before your own.
  Share the tiles, and the {fuel}, and the bad temper evenly.
  People will tell you it was a sign. It was {weather}.
  The {herd} knew it was coming; we did not. Watch the {herd}.
  It is still in my teeth. It is still in my sermon.

sermon.pest (share=0.8)
  ($e.what=locusts) The locusts came over {$e.place} like a cloud, and when they went the {crop} went with them. {med.ser.t.pest} {=.told}
  ($e.what=blight|$e.what=mildew|$e.what=worm) The {crop} is rotting in the fields at {$e.place}. {med.ser.t.pest} {=.told}
  ($e.what=murrain) The {herd} are dying at {$e.place}, standing up, with their eyes open. Do not eat the meat; I know you are hungry. {=.told}
  ($e.what=rats) The rats are in the granaries at {$e.place}, among the {vessel}. {med.ser.t.pest} {=.told}
  ($e.what=wolves) The wolves have come down to {$e.place} for the {herd}. Pen them at night, and keep the children in. {=.told}
  ($e.what=mites) The {pest} are in everything at {$e.place}. {med.ser.t.pest} {=.told}
med.ser.t.pest (share=0.6)
  It is not your sin; it is the damp. Burn what is rotten and share what is not.
  Get cats. Get many cats.
  The {bird} will eat some of them, and we will eat what the {bird} leave.
  Save the seed, whatever else you lose.
  The old ones had a prayer for this. I have forgotten it. So pray your own.

sermon.wonder (share=0.8)
  ($e.what=calf) A calf was born at {$e.place} with two heads, and the whole town went to look. {god} makes what {god} makes. Feed it. {=.told}
  ($e.what=fish) They took a fish from {river} as long as a boat. Some say it is a sign. It is a fish. Share it. {=.told}
  ($e.what=hoard) A {med.chr.trades.one} digging at {$e.place} found a pot of old coins, and now the whole family loves the {med.chr.trades.one}. {=.told}
  ($e.what=stone) A stone fell at {$e.place}, black and warm, and the priests have taken it to {holyplace}. I have seen it. It is a stone. {=.told}
  ($e.what=rain) It rained red at {$e.place}. The old ones say it has happened before. Wash your {garment.n}; it comes out. {=.told}
  ($e.what=spring) The spring at {$e.place} ran warm for a month. Some went to bathe in it and some to pray in it. It does not mind which. {=.told}
  ($e.what=beast) A white {beast} was seen at {$e.place}, and everyone has an opinion about it. The {beast} has no opinion about us. {=.told}
  ($e.what=bones) They dug up great bones at {$e.place}, older than {city}. Whatever owned them did not have a sermon either. {=.told}
  ($e.what=twins) Three children were born together at {$e.place}, and all three lived. That is a sign of a tired mother. Help her. {=.told}
  ($e.what=tree) The old {tree.n} at {$e.place} flowered in {season.now}, out of its time. Do not read it. Enjoy it. {=.told}
  ($e.what=bell) The bell at {$e.place} rang in the night with nobody at the rope. The wind, or {god}. Either way, it is fixed now. {=.told}
  ($e.what=ruin) They found old walls at {$e.place}, of the ones before us, with {motif.over} on them. They prayed too, I suppose. Pray for them. {=.told}

sermon.trial
  {med.ser.trial} {med.ser.t.trial} {=.told}
med.ser.trial
  ($e.verdict=condemned has:$e.a) {$e.a:full} was condemned at {$e.place} for {$e.what}.
  ($e.verdict=acquitted has:$e.a) {$e.a:full} was tried for {$e.what} and let go.
  ($e.verdict=exiled has:$e.a) {$e.a:full} has been sent out of the land for {$e.what}.
  ($e.verdict=fined has:$e.a) {$e.a:full} paid a fine for {$e.what}, in {coin}.
  The whole of {$e.place} went to the trial as though {festival} had come early, and brought {food}.
med.ser.t.trial (share=0.6)
  Justice is {god}'s; we only do our best with it.
  Some of you are disappointed. Be disappointed quietly.
  (has:$e.a) Somewhere a door will open for {$e.a:obj}. Pray it does.
  Paying is the easy part.
  A trial is not a feast, and the judges are not the cooks.
  Whatever you think of the verdict, the {med.chr.trades} at the back of the court were selling {food}, and that is the real sin.

sermon.law
  There is a new law on {med.law.of}. {med.ser.t.law} {=.told}
  ($e.what=tax) There is a new tax. I will not tell you to love it. I will tell you to pay it and to pray for whoever spends it. {=.told}
  ($e.what=drink) The {drink} is forbidden on the rest day now. Some of you are already looking at the door. Sit down. {=.told}
med.ser.t.law (share=0.6)
  Keep it. And keep the older law too, the one about your neighbour.
  It was always meant to be so, by {god}; now it is meant by {ruler.the} as well.
  It will be broken by {festival}, over the {drink}. Do not be the one who breaks it.
  The {med.chr.trades} are angry about it. That means it is working.
  Read it, if you can read; have it read to you if you cannot. It is cut by {town.open}.

sermon.accident
  {med.ser.acc} {med.ser.t.acc} {=.told}
med.ser.acc
  ($e.what=bridge) The bridge at {$e.place} went into the river with the carts on it.
  ($e.what=bridge) The old bridge at {$e.place} gave way under a cart of {crop}, and the cart was not the only thing it took.
  ($e.what=mine) The mine at {$e.place} came down on the men in it.
  ($e.what=mine) At {$e.place} the hill closed over the {med.chr.trades} at {time.of}, and the dogs knew before anyone.
  ($e.what=ship) The ship went down off {$e.place} with {#$e.value|soul}.
  ($e.what=ship) A ship of {$e.place} went out in {weather} and did not come back; {#$e.value|soul} on her.
  ($e.what=tower|$e.what=well) It fell at {$e.place}, and it took {#$e.value|soul} with it.
  ($e.what=tower|$e.what=well) At {$e.place} it came down in the night, {walls.of} and all, on the people sleeping by it.
  ($e.what!=bridge $e.what!=mine $e.what!=ship $e.what!=tower $e.what!=well) There was a disaster at {$e.place}, and {#$e.value|soul} are dead of it, {med.ser.accwho}.
med.ser.accwho
  most of them {med.chr.trades}
  some of them children
  and the {med.chr.trades} who tried to help
  on a working day, in good weather

med.ser.t.acc (share=0.4)
  It was nobody's fault, they say. That is what they always say. Find out, and mend it.
  Someone said it was old. Everyone knew it was old.
  Their families will be here on the rest day. Make room.
  The sea does not answer prayers. We do.
  We build, and it falls, and we build. Build it better.
  The {med.chr.trades} who did the building are sitting in their houses with the shutters closed. Go and see them; they are grieving too.
  The {med.chr.trades} of {$e.place} have wrapped their dead in {colour.mourn}, and the street is full of it.
  Carry {food} to the houses along {streets.of}; there are empty places at those tables now.
  Next time someone says it is old and will hold, ask them how old, and walk round.

sermon.fair
  {med.ser.fair} {med.ser.t.fair} {=.told}
med.ser.fair
  The fair at {$e.place} brought half the country, and the cutpurses brought the other half.
  ($e.what=beasts|$e.what=horses) They sold {herd} at the fair at {$e.place} for more than a year's wages.
  You have all been to the fair at {$e.place}, and come back with {med.ser.fairbuy} and without your {coin}.
  ($e.what=cloth) The cloth fair at {$e.place} was all {colour.any} this year, and so are your daughters.
  ($e.what=wine) The wine fair at {$e.place} has given half of you a headache and the other half a debt.
med.ser.fairbuy
  {colour.any} ribbons
  a {vessel.one.n} you did not need
  a {beast} that will not stay penned
  {fruit} from the far country
  a new {garment.n}

med.ser.t.fair (share=0.5)
  Buy honestly; sell honestly.
  Remember who fed them.
  That is what fairs are for.
  The {med.chr.trades} of {$home} sold well there, and I expect to see it in the tithe.
  Next year, bring back fewer ribbons and more {crop2} seed.

sermon.games
  (has:$e.a) {$e.a:full} won {med.games.of} at {$e.place}, and the whole town carried {$e.a:obj} home. Carry the losers home too; they ran as far. {=.told}
  There were games at {$e.place}, and nobody was killed, which is better than last time. {=.told}
  The young men of {$home} have been racing the {herd} again. Let them; it is better than the other things young men do with {drink}. {=.told}
  The games at {$e.place} were won by somebody's son and lost by everybody else's. {med.ser.t.games} {=.told}
med.ser.t.games
  That is the way of it.
  The {herd} did not care, and neither does {god}.
  Go and congratulate them, and then go back to work.

sermon.visit
  {$e.a:full} came to {$e.place}, and the streets were swept for the first time since the founding. Keep them swept. {=.told}
  {$e.a:full} came to {$e.place} and did not come here. {god} is not offended; I am, a little. {=.told}
  {$e.a:full} came to {$e.place}, and the {med.chr.trades} put out {colour.holy} cloth and raised their prices. {=.told}
sermon.price
  ($e.out=1) The price of {$e.what} has gone up again. Somebody is happy about it. Find out who, and ask them for some. {=.told}
  ($e.out=0) The price of {$e.what} has fallen, and the sellers are crying in the market. Buy from them anyway. {=.told}
  ($e.out=1) {$e.what} costs more this {season.now} than it did when I was young, and I was young a long time ago. {=.told}
sermon.embassy
  ($e.what=others) Envoys of the {other} came to {$e.place}. They bowed the wrong way and ate the wrong things and were very polite. Be polite back. {=.told}
  ($e.what=city) Envoys came from {$e.place2} to {$e.place} with gifts. Gifts always want something. So do prayers. {=.told}
  ($e.what=sent) We have sent envoys to the {other}, with {med.ser.gifts}. Pray they come back, and with something better. {=.told}
med.ser.gifts
  {colour.holy} cloth
  {crop} and {drink}
  the best {craft} we have
sermon.show
  There is a new {med.show.of} at {$e.place}, and everyone is talking about it. Good. It means you have something to talk about besides each other. {=.told}
  (has:$e.a) {$e.a:full} has made a {med.show.of} at {$e.place}, and some of you have seen it twice. {god} has seen it once, and liked it. {=.told}

)VG"
